  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Renting out your apartment or home for a few weeks or months can be a smart way to cover costs while you travel, work elsewhere, or spend the summer away. But in many places, the rules for renting out a home short-term are different from the rules for a standard lease, and they have been changing over the past several years.</p>
<p>This article is a general overview of the kinds of rules hosts commonly run into. It is not legal or tax advice, and it does not describe the law in any particular city or state. Rules vary widely and change often. Before you list, check your lease or ownership documents and your local laws, and talk to a professional if you are unsure.</p>

<h2>Why the length of stay matters</h2>
<p>Many cities and some states treat rentals differently depending on how long the guest stays. A common dividing line is 30 days: stays shorter than that are often classified as "short-term rentals" or "transient" occupancy and may be subject to extra rules, permits, or taxes. Stays of 30 days or longer are often treated more like ordinary residential tenancies.</p>
<p>That said, 30 days is not universal. Some places use a different threshold, and some regulate longer stays too. Do not assume a monthly rental is automatically exempt from local rules. Look up the definitions in your city or county code.</p>
<p>This is one reason many hosts on The Sublet focus on stays of a month or longer, which are a natural fit for summer sublets, interns, travel nurses, and people relocating. But "a month or longer" does not by itself guarantee compliance, so still check.</p>

<h2>The layers of rules to check</h2>
<p>Think of short-term rental rules as several layers. Any one of them can prohibit or restrict what you want to do.</p>

<h3>1. Your lease (if you rent)</h3>
<p>If you are a tenant, your lease comes first. Many leases restrict subletting, require landlord approval, or prohibit short-term rentals entirely. Violating the lease can put your tenancy at risk, even if the city itself would allow the rental. Our guide on <a href="/blog/how-to-sublet-your-apartment-legally/">how to sublet your apartment legally</a> covers getting permission, and <a href="/blog/subletting-vs-assigning-a-lease/">subletting vs. assigning a lease</a> explains the difference between the two arrangements.</p>

<h3>2. HOA, condo, or co-op rules (if you own)</h3>
<p>Owning your home does not mean you can rent it however you like. Homeowners associations, condo associations, and co-op boards often have rules about rentals: minimum lease lengths, caps on how many units can be rented at once, approval processes for tenants, registration with the board, or outright bans on short-term stays. These rules are usually found in the governing documents (often called CC&amp;Rs, bylaws, or house rules). Read them, and ask the board or management company if anything is unclear.</p>

<h3>3. City or county rules</h3>
<p>Local governments are where most short-term rental regulation happens. Common types of rules include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Registration or permits:</strong> Some places require hosts to register the rental, get a permit or license, and display the registration number on listings.</li>
<li><strong>Primary residence requirements:</strong> Some places only allow short-term rentals in a host's primary residence, or only when the host is present.</li>
<li><strong>Caps on nights:</strong> Some places limit how many nights per year a home can be rented short-term.</li>
<li><strong>Zoning limits:</strong> Short-term rentals may be allowed in some zones and not others.</li>
<li><strong>Safety requirements:</strong> Smoke and carbon monoxide detectors, fire extinguishers, posted emergency information, and occupancy limits are common.</li>
<li><strong>Inspections:</strong> Some permits require an inspection before approval.</li>
</ul>
<p>Penalties for violating local rules can be significant in some places. Your city or county website is usually the best starting point; search for terms like "short-term rental," "transient occupancy," or "home sharing."</p>

<h3>4. State rules</h3>
<p>Some states have their own rules on short-term rentals, lodging taxes, or landlord-tenant obligations. State law can also affect what local governments are allowed to regulate. A quick search of your state's revenue department and housing agency websites is a good start.</p>

<h3>5. Building rules</h3>
<p>Even in a rental building where subletting is allowed, the building may have its own requirements: registering guests, issuing fobs, moving-day reservations, or elevator booking. Check with management.</p>

<h2>Taxes to be aware of</h2>
<p>Taxes are one of the most overlooked parts of hosting. Again, this is general information, not tax advice, and you should check with a tax professional about your own situation.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Income tax:</strong> Rental income is generally taxable income in the US. There are rules about what counts, what expenses can be deducted, and special situations for very short rental periods. A tax professional can tell you how they apply to you.</li>
<li><strong>Occupancy or lodging taxes:</strong> Many states and localities charge taxes on short stays, similar to hotel taxes. Whether they apply often depends on the length of stay. Some platforms collect these taxes for hosts in some areas, but you should not assume that happens for every listing or every platform.</li>
<li><strong>Sales tax:</strong> In some places, short-term lodging is subject to sales tax.</li>
</ul>
<p>Keep good records: dates rented, amounts received, and expenses like cleaning, repairs, and utilities. Records make tax time easier, whatever the rules turn out to be.</p>

<h2>Insurance</h2>
<p>Standard renters and homeowners insurance policies may not cover damage or liability related to renting your home to someone else, especially for short stays. Call your insurer, explain what you plan to do, and ask whether you need additional coverage or a different policy. Get the answer in writing if you can.</p>

<h2>Fair housing applies to hosts</h2>
<p>Federal fair housing law, along with many state and local laws, prohibits discrimination in housing based on protected characteristics. Some exemptions exist for certain owner-occupied situations, but they are narrow, and state or local laws may be stricter. The safe and right approach is simple: choose subtenants based on things like dates, budget, references, and how well your expectations match, and treat every inquiry the same way. Write your listing to describe the space, not the kind of person you want.</p>

<h2>A practical checklist before you list</h2>
<ol>
<li>Read your lease or HOA/condo documents for rental restrictions.</li>
<li>Get written permission from your landlord or board if required.</li>
<li>Look up your city and county rules for the length of stay you plan to offer.</li>
<li>Find out whether you need a permit, registration, or license, and whether a number must appear in your listing.</li>
<li>Check state and local occupancy tax rules.</li>
<li>Call your insurer.</li>
<li>Make sure smoke and CO detectors work and safety information is posted.</li>
<li>Use a written <a href="/blog/sublease-agreement-what-to-include/">sublease or rental agreement</a>.</li>
<li>Keep records of income and expenses.</li>
</ol>

<h2>When to get professional help</h2>
<p>If your situation is complicated, such as renting out multiple properties, renting a condo with unclear rules, or operating in a city with strict regulations, a local real estate attorney or tax professional is worth the cost. A one-time consultation is cheap compared with fines or a lease violation.</p>

<h2>Listing on The Sublet</h2>
<p>Once you have confirmed you can rent your place, you can <a href="/post-a-sublet/">post a sublet</a> for free. Free listings are reviewed before publishing, and renters contact you through a relay form so your email stays private. For help setting the right price, see our <a href="/blog/how-to-price-your-sublet/">pricing guide</a>, and for the listing itself, read <a href="/blog/how-to-write-a-sublet-listing-that-rents/">how to write a sublet listing that rents</a>.</p>
<p>The rules can feel like a lot. But checking them once, up front, means you can host with confidence rather than worrying about a letter from your landlord, board, or city.</p>
